\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 1\ulnone . Introduction\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1\b0 1.1. Purpose\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 The purpose of this specification is to define a lossless compressed data format that :\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-142\li994\sl240\slmult1 Is independent of CPU type, operation system, file system, and character set, and hence can be used for interchange;\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Can compress or decompress a data stream (as opposed to a randomly accessible file) to produce another data stream, using only an a priori bounded amount of intermediate storage, and hence can be used in data communications or similar structures such as Unix filters;\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Compresses data with efficiency comparable to the best currently available general-purpose compression methods, and in particular considerably better that the "compress" program;\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Can be implemented readily in a manner not covered by patents, and hence can be practiced freely;\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Is compatible with the file format produced by the current widely used gzip utility, in that conforming decompressors will be able to read data produced by the existing gzip compressor.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 The data format defined by this specification does not attempt to :\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-142\li994\sl240\slmult1 Provide random access to compressed data;\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Compress specialized data (e.g., raster graphics) as well as the best currently available specialized algorithms.\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1 1.2. Intended audience\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 This specification is intended for use by implementors of software to compress data into gzip gormat and/or decompress datat from gzip format.\par
The text of the specification assumes a basic background in programming at the level of bits and other primitive data representations.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1 1.3. Scope\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 The specification specifies a compression method and a file format (the latter assuming only that a file can store a sequence of arbitrary bytes). It does not specify any particular interfacr to a file system or anything about character sets or encodings (except for file names and comments, which are optional).\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1 1.4. Compliance\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 Unless otherwise indicated below, a compliant decompressor must be able to accept and decompress any file that conforms to all the specifications presented here; a compliant compressor must produce files that conform to all the specifications presented here. The material in the appendices is not part of the specification per se and is not relevant to compliance.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1 1.5. Definitions of terms and conventions used\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 byte : 8 bits stored or transmissed as a unit (same as an octet). (For this specification, a byte is exactly 8 bits, even on machines which store a character on a number of bits different from 8.) See below for the numbering of bits within a byte.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1 1.6. Changes from previous versions\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 There have been to technical changes to the gzip format since version 4.1 of this specification. In version 4.2, some terminology was changed, and the sample CRC code was rewritten for clarity and to eliminate the requirement for the caller to do pre- and post-conditioning. Version 4.3 is a conversion of the specification to RFC style.\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\lang1041\par

\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b\lang1033 2\ulnone . Detailed specification\b0\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1 2.1. Overall conventions\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 In the diagrams below, a box like this :\par
