\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1017 
\pard\intbl\cell\row 
\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1\lang1049 represents one byte; a box like this :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1017\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1182\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1347\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1512\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1677\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1842\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2007\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2172 
\pard\intbl\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\cell\row 
\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 represent a variable number of bytes.\par
Bytes stored within a computer do not have a "bit order", since they are always treated as a unit. However, a byte considered as an integer between 0 and 255 does have a most- and least-significant bit, and since we write numbers with the most-significant digit on the left, we also write bytes with the most-significant bit on the left. In the diagrams below, we numbr the bits of a byte so that bit 0 is the least-significant bit, i. e., the bits are numbered :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1857 
\pard\intbl\f1 76543210\cell\row 
\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1\f0 This document does not address the issue of the order in which bits of a byte are transmissed on a bit-sequential medium, since the data format described here is byte- rather than bit-oriented.\par
Within a computer, a number may occupt multiple bytes. All multi-byte numbers in the format described here are stored with the least-significant byte first (at the lower memory address). For example, the decimal number 520 is stored as :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1902\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2942 
\pard\intbl\qc\f1 0\cell 1\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft852\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1902\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2942 
\pard\intbl\qc\lang1033 00001000\par

\pard\intbl ^\par
|\par
|\par
+ less significant byte = 8\cell 
\pard\intbl\qc 00000010\par

\pard\intbl ^\par
|\par
+ more significant byte = 2 * 256\cell\row 
