\pard\li568\sl360\slmult1 2.3.1. Member header and trailer\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 ID1 (IDentification 1)\par
ID2 (IDentification 2)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1 These have the fixed valies ID1 = 31 (0x1f, \\037), ID2 = 139 (0x8b, \\213), to identify the file as being in gzip format.\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 CM (Compression Method)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1 This identifies the compression method used in the file. CM = 0-7 are reserved. CM = 8 denotes the "deflate" compression method, which is the one customarily used by gzip and which is documented elsewhere.\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1 FLG (FLaGs)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1 This flag byte is divided into individual bits as follows :\par

\pard\li1420\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 bit 0 FTEXT\par
bit 1 FHCRC\par
bit 2 FEXTRA\par
bit 3 FNAME\par
bit 4 FCOMMENT\par
bit 5 reserved\par
bit 6 reserved\par
bit 7 reserved\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 If FTEXT is set, the file is probably ASCII text. This is an optional indication, which the compressor may set by checking a small amount of the input data to see whether any non-ASCII characters are present. In case of doubt, FTEXT is cleared, indicating binary data. For systems which have different file formats for ascii text and binary data, the decompressor can use FTEXT to choose the appropriate format. We deliberately do not specify the algorithm used to set this bit, since a compressot always has the option os leaving it cleared and a decompressor always has the option of ignoring it and letting some other program handle issues of data conversion.\par
If FHCRC is set, a CRC16 for the gzip header is present immediately before the compressed data. The CRC16 consists of the two least significant bytes of the CRC32 for all bytes of the gzip header up to and not including the CRC16. [The FHCRC bit was never set by versions of gzip up to 1.2.4, even though it was documented with a different meaning in gzip 1.2.4.]\par
If FEXTRA is set, optional extra fields are present, as described in a following section.\par
If FNAME is set, an original name is present, terminated by a zero byte. The name must consist of ISO 8859-1 (LATIN-1) characters; on operating system using EBCDIC or any other character set for file names, the namr, must be translated to the ISO LATIN-1 character set. This is the original name of the file being compressed with any directory components removed, and, if the file being compressed is on a file system with case insensitive names, forced to lower case. There is no original file name if the data was compressed from a source other than a named file; for example, if the source was stdin on a Unix system, there is no file name.\par
If FCOMMENT is set, a zero-terminated file comment is present. This comment is not interpreted;; it is only intended for human consumption. The comment must consist of ISO 8859-1 (LATIN-1) characters. Line breaks should be denoted by a single line feed character (10 decimal).\par
Reserved FLG bits must be zero.\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 MTIME (Modification TIME)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 This gives the most recent modification time of the original gile being compressed. The time is in Unix format, i.e., seconds since 00:00:00 GMT, Jan. 1, 1970. (Note that this may cause problems for MS-DOS and other systems that use local rather than Universal time.) If the compressed data did not come a file, MTIME is set to the time at which compression started. MTIME  = 0 means no time stamp is available.\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 XFL (eXtra FLags)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 These flags are available for use by specific compression methods. The "deflate" method (CM = 8) sets these flags as follows :\par

\pard\li1420\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 XFL = 2 - compressor used maximum compression, slowest algorithm\par
XFL = 4 - compressor used fastest algorithm\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 OS (Operating System)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 This identifies the type of file system on which compression took place. This may useful in determining end-of-line convention for text files. The currently defined values are as follows :\par

\pard\li1420\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 0 - FAT filesystem (MS-DOS, OS/2, NT/Win32)\par
1 - Amiga\par
2 - VMS (or OpenVMS)\par
3 - Unix\par
4 - VM/CMS\par
5 - Atari TOS\par
6 - HPFS filesystem (OS/2, NT)\par
7 - Macintosh\par
8 - Z-System\par
9 - CP/M\par
10 - TOPS-20\par
11 - NTFS filesystem (NT)\par
12 - QDOS\par
13 - Acorn RISCOS\par
255 - unknown\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 XLEN (eXtra LENgth)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 If FLG.FEXTRA is set, this gives the length of the optional extra field. See below for details.\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 CRC32\lang1033  (CRC-32)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 This contains a Cyclic Redudancy Check value of the uncompressed data conputed according to CRC-32 algorithm used in the ISO 3309 standard and in \ul section 8.1.1.6.2\ulnone  of ITU-T recommendation V.42. (See \ul http://www.iso.ch\ulnone  for otdering ISO documents. See gopher://info.itu.ch for an online version of ITU-T V.42.)\par

\pard\li852\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 ISIZE (Input SIZE)\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 This contains the size of the original (uncompressed) input data modulo 2\super 32\nosupersub .\par
\par

\pard\li852\sl360\slmult1\tx1136 2.3.1.1. Extra field\par

\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136 If the FLG.FEXTRA bit is set, an "extra field" is present in the header, with total length XLEN. It consists of a series of subfields, each of the form :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1420\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx1915\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2370\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2850\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx6560 
\pard\intbl\f1 SI1\cell SI2\cell LEN\cell ... LEN bytes of subfield data...\cell\row 
\pard\li1136\sl240\slmult1\tx1136\f0\lang1049 SI1 and SI2 provide a subfield ID, typically two ASCII letters with some mnemonic value. Jean-Loup Gailly <gzip@prep.ai.mit.edu> is maintaining a registry of subfield IDs; please send him any subfield UD you wish to use. Subfield IDs with SI2 = 0 are reserved for future use. The following IDs are currently defined :\par
\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1420\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx2650\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx3885\clbrdrl\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrt\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrw1\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrw1\brdrs \cellx7020 
\pard\intbl\f1 SI1\cell SI2\cell Data\cell\row\trowd\trgaph72\trleft1420\trpaddl72\trpaddr72\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3
